# Line endings: LF, everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# Same policy and same spelling as z80fpga, which has had this file for a while:
# `text=auto eol=lf`.  Git stores LF and checks out LF, so there is one answer on
# every platform and no tool can disagree with another about what a line ends
# with.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE.  Nothing declared line endings in any of these repositories,
# and a number of tracked files were CRLF by accident of having been written on
# Windows years ago.  A script that writes '\n' - which is what Python's
# open(p, "w") does - then rewrites every line in such a file.  That happened
# four times in one day in romwbw_emu, each time producing a commit whose
# diffstat claimed hundreds of insertions for an edit of a dozen lines.  It is
# not cosmetic: a diff like that cannot be reviewed, and `git blame` afterwards
# points at the line-ending change rather than at whoever wrote the line.
#
# Declaring the files `-text` would have documented the hazard and prevented
# nothing, because git would still commit the flattened bytes.  Storing LF is
# what makes the failure impossible: a tool that writes LF now changes nothing.
#
# To find CRLF files again if this is ever undone:
#   git ls-files | while read -r f; do
#       n=$(tr -cd '\r' < "$f" | wc -c); [ "$n" -gt 0 ] && echo "$f $n"; done
* text=auto eol=lf

# tests/8080/ is left exactly as it is.  The .mac and .prn files there are
# Frank Cringle's 8080/Z80 exerciser as converted by Ian Bartholomew - a
# third-party artifact kept beside the .com the suite actually runs, for
# provenance.  Their CRLF is how they were published, and run_tests.sh reads
# none of them.
tests/8080/** -text

# Binaries, declared rather than left to git's NUL-byte guess.
*.com        binary
*.bin        binary
*.png        binary
